Statements:
[
    "Vibe testing is basically a quick, informal way of checking whether an LLM‑driven application seems to work.",
    "Imagine you sit down, fire off five or ten prompts that come to mind, read the answers, and then decide “it feels right” if the responses look good.",
    "The whole process is driven by that gut feeling—you’re judging the output by how it feels rather than by any systematic criteria.",
    "Because it relies on a handful of ad‑hoc questions, vibe testing is highly subjective.",
    "What feels correct today might not feel the same a week later, especially since you probably won’t repeat exactly the same set of prompts.",
    "In other words, it isn’t repeatable: you can’t reliably reproduce the test or compare results across runs.",
    "That makes it suitable only for personal tinkering or a quick sanity check, not for the rigor that production software demands.",
    "When you try to launch an LLM application into a real product, you need more than a pleasant “vibe.”",
    "You need methodological evaluation—structured test suites, coverage of edge cases, quantitative metrics, and repeatable procedures that give you confidence the model will behave correctly under a wide range of inputs.",
    "Relying solely on vibe testing means you might miss systematic errors, biases, or failure modes that only show up outside your small, hand‑picked prompt set.",
    "As a result, teams that have deployed an LLM app after only vibe testing have often run into problems because the informal check never proved the system robust enough for production use."
] 

