    "Adding more data does not automatically make a model more knowledgeable in every case.",
    "The benchmark MMLU gauges how smart a model is by focusing on breadth across many subjects.",
    "MMLU was created to measure what the model has actually learned, not just how much data it has seen.",
    "The benchmark evaluates knowledge across 57 subjects and 14,000 multiple‑choice questions from real exams and textbooks.",
    "The need for such a test indicates that more data alone isn’t a guarantee of better performance.",
    "Models must be checked for their ability to retrieve and apply knowledge correctly.",
    "Training on a larger, diverse dataset generally gives a model more material to absorb, which can increase its knowledge capability, but it isn’t a universal rule.",
    "The ultimate measure is how well the model performs on evaluations like MMLU, which assess breadth and indirectly depth of knowledge.",
    "More data can help, but other factors—model architecture, data quality and variety, and training methods—also play crucial roles in determining a final LLM’s knowledgeability."
] 

