Claims:
[
    "When you put a chatbot into production, the most useful logs are the ones that let you reconstruct exactly what happened in each turn and also give you enough data to spot problems or improve the system later.",
    "In plain language, think of a log as a diary entry for every interaction: you want to record not just what the user said and what the bot replied, but also the “behind‑the‑scenes” details that tell you how the request was processed.",
    "First, capture the user’s input and the exact question you asked the retrieval‑augmented generation (RAG) system.",
    "That way you know what the user was trying to get at, and you can later compare it with the context that the system pulled in order to answer.",
    "Next, store the context that the RAG component fetched – essentially the set of documents or snippets that were fed to the language model to produce the answer.",
    "Together with the context, keep the model’s output (the response that was sent back to the user).",
    "Having the three pieces—user query, retrieved context, and bot answer—lets you replay any conversation and understand why a particular response was generated.",
    "Beyond the content of the conversation, it’s important to log operational metrics.",
    "Latency in milliseconds tells you how long the request took, which is useful for performance monitoring.",
    "Token counts—how many tokens were used in the prompt and how many were generated as completion—let you track usage and cost, and you should also record the total monetary cost of the call.",
    "If anything went wrong, capture the error information, including the status code, so you can diagnose failures quickly.",
    "Finally, don’t forget the behavioral signals that give you a sense of the user’s satisfaction or escalation.",
    "If the user clicks a thumbs‑up or thumbs‑down, logs that reaction.",
    "If the user asks to speak to a human, provides an email address, or otherwise indicates that the conversation is escalating, record that intent.",
    "Repeated or re‑phrased questions are also worth noting, because they often signal that the bot isn’t meeting the user’s needs.",
    "In short, a good production log should include: the user’s original question, the retrieved context the bot used, the bot’s response, performance data such as latency and token usage, cost information, any error codes if the request failed, user feedback signals (thumbs up/down, escalation requests, email contacts), and patterns like repeated or re‑phrased queries.",
    "Tools like Langsmith already provide a convenient place to store all of these pieces together, organizing them by project so you can browse full conversation histories, see the metadata, and run analyses later.",
    "By capturing each of these elements, you’ll have a complete picture of what happened in production and the data you need to improve the system over time."
] 

